window.SKILLS={"built":"2026-10-04","skills":[{"id":"nexi-video","name":"nexi-video","desc":"Make, fix, redesign or audit TechNext explainer videos starring Nexi, the 3D robot mascot, with the in-house renderer at ~/ClaudeWork/nexi-tutorial. Covers Odoo walkthroughs, topic videos (myth-busting, quick tip, module explainer, before/after), solution videos (one per technext.asia service, built on an analogy or role-play), and any new Nexi video. Output is a 16:9 MP4 with Nexi's robot voice, typed captions, music and sound effects, delivered to the shared drive. Use whenever the user asks for a Nexi video, a video about an Odoo app or a TechNext service, or a fix or audit of an existing Nexi video.","group":"video","own":true,"licence":"","home":"","note":"","version":"","invoke":"auto","nfiles":7,"date":"2026-10-04","docs":[{"path":"SKILL.md","kind":"md","text":"# Nexi videos\n\nTechNext makes cinematic explainer videos starring Nexi, the 3D robot from technext.asia. Every frame is rendered in headless Chrome by the in-house engine at `~/ClaudeWork/nexi-tutorial`. Nothing is hand-animated or screen-recorded. A video is a JavaScript \"film\" script: timed captions in Nexi's robot voice, plus props, camera moves and Nexi's acting.\n\n## Before anything else\n\n1. **Read the history.** Read `reference/lessons.md` in this skill. Every past mistake and fix is logged there; do not repeat them.\n2. **Read the guides.**\n   - `reference/design-rules.md` covers how a frame must look.\n   - `reference/engine.md` is the API.\n   - `reference/workflow.md` has the exact commands.\n3. **Check what exists.**\n   - The memory file `project_nexi_odoo_walkthrough.md` lists what has been delivered.\n   - `~/ClaudeWork/nexi-tutorial/web/films/` holds every existing film. Copy the closest one rather than starting blank. `reference/film-template.js` is the skeleton.\n4. **Get the facts right.**\n   - Take content from the live website repo (`~/ClaudeWork/TechNext-Website`, branch `origin/master`). Each solution page states what TechNext really offers.\n   - Never invent claims, prices, client names or office locations.\n   - TechNext is Singapore HQ, with teams in the Philippines and Vietnam, and delivered in 10+ countries. There is no Malaysia office.\n   - Sample companies must be clearly fictional and labelled \"sample\".\n\n## The pipeline (one video)\n\n1. **Concept.** One analogy or role-play world per video, unique across the series. Nexi wears a costume that fits (`wear()`). Plan:\n   - 8\u201313 caption lines, about 50\u201375 s in total;\n   - one viewer quiz with a countdown;\n   - at least three hands-on moments (grab, toss, stamp, pull, paint, ride\u2026);\n   - one random charming element (a bird, a cow, a bee, a cursor with eyes);\n   - a unique call-to-action object;\n   - an intro badge, an outro badge and an outro spot (`outroSeq({ nexi })`) not used by the previous video. Pick the spot for the hat: tall hats (chef toque, knight plume) clip at `top` and poke into the CTA row at `below`; `below` suits low hats only.\n2. **Script.** Write `web/films/<name>.js`. Every caption is `say()`, and every beat hangs off a caption: `at(line.w('word'), \u2026)`. Keep lines speakable and truthful. Do not start a line with \"...\" (the voice pauses on dots).\n3. **Build and probe.** `python build_film.py <name>`, then `render.py probe`. Fix any load error before going further.\n   - `build_film.py` refuses to build a film that redeclares an engine top-level name (a film's `var` re-assigns the engine's variable; `var FLY` once froze every flight). Rename and rebuild.\n   - The probe prints the caption timeline (lines start with spaces, so don't filter them out with `grep -v '^ '`).\n4. **Contact sheet.** Render a sheet at every beat and at every transition (mid-flight, mid-camera-move frames too). Audit each frame against `reference/qa-checklist.md`. Fix, re-sheet the changed beats, and repeat until clean.\n5. **Render.** `render.py mp4`. You can run several films at once with different ports.\n6. **Audio QA.** Run `qa_av.py` and `qa/_buslevel.py`. Targets:\n   - about \u221217 LUFS;\n   - true peak \u2264 \u22122 dBFS;\n   - voice ratio \u2265 3.5\u00d7;\n   - music 10\u201312 dB under the voice and about 5 dB under it in the gaps.\n   Fix by re-muxing the audio only.\n7. **Final frame check.** `deliver.py sheet <name>` pulls 12 frames from the encoded MP4. Look at them.\n8. **Deliver.** Copy the MP4 to the shared drive (md5-checked) and send it to the user straight away.\n   - Run `deliver.py copy <name>`; it copies to the shared drive and verifies the md5.\n   - Then send the file to the user (SendUserFile, status proactive) the moment it is ready. Do not batch.\n   - Captions say what the video shows and how long it runs.\n9. **Report.** Give a status table across the batch: video, length, state.\n10. **Keep this skill alive.** Log the lessons, promote new rules and republish the hub skills page (see below). This is not optional.\n\n## Non-negotiables (from user feedback)\n\n- **Nothing in a corner.** Nexi, cards and props sit in balanced compositions. Nexi never sits behind a card, a quiz or the caption, and never hugs a frame edge. In the end card she uses one of the outro spots, a different one per video.\n- **Text must be readable.**\n  - On screen, important text is at least 22 px and labels at least 18 px, measured after camera zoom: world size \u00d7 `CAM.zoom`.\n  - Never let text overlap other text, Nexi, or another prop's text.\n  - Fit labels inside their boxes, or wrap them.\n- **Lively but clean.**\n  - Nexi roams the whole set: `fly`, `hop`, ride things, carry things.\n  - Reactions are exaggerated (`RX.*`, `E.*`).\n  - The camera moves (push-ins, whip pans, tracking, crane, dutch tilt) and is still while text is being read.\n- **Logos and branding.**\n  - The intro and end-card logo is centred.\n  - \"with Nexi\" sits as in the walkthrough.\n  - Use the plain Odoo logo, never \"Odoo Ready Partner\".\n  - Marketing videos use the marketing end card: Websites \u00b7 Social \u00b7 Brand, no Odoo logo.\n- **Music:** upbeat, one style per topic, never loud.\n- **Format:** 16:9 only for now. The user postponed 9:16.\n- **Tell the user as each video finishes.** Finish each video's QA and delivery the moment its render completes.\n\n## Keep this skill alive\n\nAfter every video job, whether new videos, fixes or audits:\n\n1. **Log what you learned.** Append dated entries to `reference/lessons.md`: what went wrong, why, and the fix or rule. Include user feedback verbatim when it set a rule.\n2. **Promote rules.** If a lesson is a new rule, add it to `reference/design-rules.md` or to the non-negotiables above.\n3. **Document new APIs.** If you added engine features (a new helper, quiz layout or outro spot), document them in `reference/engine.md`.\n4. **Republish the hub page.** Run `python ~/ClaudeWork/technext-marketing-hub/tools/build_skills.py`, then commit and push only `skills.html`, `assets/skills/` and `tools/build_skills.py`. The hub clone is shared with other chats, so use explicit `git add`, never `-A`. Check that https://technextsg.github.io/technext-marketing-hub/skills.html returns 200.\n5. **Update memory.** Update `project_nexi_odoo_walkthrough.md` with what was delivered.\n"},{"path":"reference/workflow.md","kind":"md","text":"# Workflow: files, commands, delivery\n\n## Where things live\n\n| Path | What |\n|---|---|\n| `~/ClaudeWork/nexi-tutorial/` | The renderer (git-less working folder; mirrored to Drive `_source/` folders) |\n| `web/nexi-core.js` | Nexi's 3D model and acting. Generated by `build_core.py` from `~/ClaudeWork/nexi-cute/web/nexi-core.js`. Edit `build_core.py`, never the output. |\n| `web/lib.js` | Helpers: easing, tweens, text, pills, particles, Odoo app tiles (`L.drawTile`), `L.ODOO` icon list |\n| `web/set.js` | The 3D stage: grid floor, motes, blurred app tiles, the floating Odoo window (`P`, `SET.PBASE`) |\n| `web/ui.js` | Stylised Odoo screens drawn on a canvas (CRM, quote, delivery, invoice, report, replenish\u2026) with hotspots `UI.spots` |\n| `web/intro.js` | The website logo intro and the end card (`Intro.slotL/slotR` badge slots) |\n| `web/engine_head.js` | Timeline, voice + captions, expressions `E`, reactions `RX`, HUD, chapters, intro/outro sequences, zaps, spotlight, lens, tether, badge |\n| `web/engine_v2.js` | Camera, roaming, things, costumes, quiz, `L.ICON` vector icons, marketing end card |\n| `web/engine_kit.js` | Drawing kit `KIT.*` (cards, chips, people, arrows, stamps, paperwork) and role icons |\n| `web/engine_tail.js` | Frame composer: every layer in order, camera transforms, `F(i)` |\n| `web/films/<name>.js` | One film per file: props + script |\n| `audio_t.py` | Robot voice, music styles (`STYLES`, `MAIN` preset map, `MUSIC_GAIN`), sound effects `S()` |\n| `render.py` | `probe`, `sheet`, `shots`, `mp4` (`NAMES` = output file names, `LIMIT` = limiter per film) |\n| `deliver.py` | `sheet` (12 frames from the encoded MP4) and `copy` (to Drive + md5); `SRC`/`DST` maps |\n| `mux.py` | Rebuild only the soundtrack of a rendered MP4 |\n| `qa_av.py`, `qa/_buslevel.py`, `qa/_peaks.py` | Audio QA (loudness, peak and voice ratio; bus levels; which effect causes a peak or fills a gap) |\n| `sync_source.py` | Mirror the source into the Drive `_source/` folders (20., 21., 22.) |\n| `patches/pNN_*.py` | Every engine change as a re-runnable patch (assert-and-replace) |\n\nDelivery folders (Google Drive `G:\\Shared drives\\Marketing\\00. TechNext Folder\\`):\n`20. Nexi Odoo Walkthrough`, `21. Nexi Odoo Topic Videos`, `22. Nexi Solution Videos` (+ new numbered folders for new series).\n\n## Commands (bash, from `~/ClaudeWork/nexi-tutorial`)\n\n```\npython build_film.py <name>                                  # stitch the film bundle\nNT_VIDEO=<name> python render.py probe                       # load check + caption timeline\nNT_VIDEO=<name> python render.py sheet 6.5,9.4,12.0 r1        # contact sheet qa/<name>-r1.jpg (renders every frame up to the last time)\nNT_VIDEO=<name> python render.py shots 41.2,42.6              # full-size frames qa/f-<t>.jpg\nNT_VIDEO=<name> NT_PORT=3990 CDP_PORT=9390 CDP_PROFILE=profile-nt-x python render.py mp4 > out/render-<name>.log 2>&1   # final (run in background)\npython qa_av.py out/<file>.mp4 events-<name>.json             # LUFS, true peak, voice ratio\npython qa/_buslevel.py <name>                                 # music under voice / in gaps\npython qa/_peaks.py <name>                                    # where the true peaks are + what fills each gap\npython deliver.py sheet <name>                                # look before copying\npython deliver.py copy <name>                                 # Drive + md5\npython sync_source.py                                         # after the batch\n```\n\nNew film: add it to `render.py NAMES`, `deliver.py SRC` + `DST`, `audio_t.MAIN` (music preset) and, if needed, `render.LIMIT` (use 0.7).\nAudio-only fix: `ffmpeg -i out/<f>.mp4 -an -c:v copy out/<f>.v.mp4 && python mux.py <name>`, then delete `.v.mp4` and `.wav`.\n\nRendering runs at ~20 fps per Chrome; several films can render at once (each with its own `NT_PORT`, `CDP_PORT`, `CDP_PROFILE`). A sheet up to 60 s takes ~40 s. If a render dies with \"F is not defined\", something outside killed the page; rerun.\n\n## Writing patches and films (tooling gotchas)\n\n- Bash heredocs that contain JavaScript break. Write JS and patch scripts with the Write tool, then run them.\n- The Write tool turns `\\u2019`-style escapes into the literal character. That is fine in JS, but patch match strings must use the literal character too.\n- Patches use assert-and-replace (`assert s.count(a) == 1`), so a stale match fails loudly instead of silently.\n- Never `git add -A` in the shared hub clone.\n\n## Reporting\n\n- **After each video:** send the MP4 with a caption of 2\u20134 sentences: what happens, the length, and anything notable.\n- **After the batch:** a status table, one line per fix, and anything still open, such as a pending category, missing facts or a question.\n"},{"path":"reference/design-rules.md","kind":"md","text":"# Design rules (every frame)\n\nThese came from user audits. Breaking any of them is a defect.\n\n## Frame and safe areas (screen px, 1920\u00d71080)\n- **The HUD** sits top-left (x < 260, y < 140). Nothing important goes under it. Pops are clamped away from it.\n- **The caption card** is 1180 px wide, bottom-centred, with its top edge at about y 850\u2013880. Nothing the viewer must read sits behind it. Nexi's body stays above y \u2248 830 unless she is deliberately rising from the bottom.\n- **Margins:** keep subjects 120 px or more from the left and right edges. Frame edges are not resting places.\n- **Caption height:** the caption's top edge is at about y 880 for one line, 835 for two and 720 for three. Keep anything the viewer must see above it, or shorten the line. Two lines hold about 86 characters.\n- **Composition:**\n  - Default pair: Nexi centre-left (~470) with the card centre-right (~1215), or the mirror image.\n  - With three elements (Nexi, card, prop), spread them across thirds.\n  - Never park a card or Nexi in a corner, including in end cards.\n\n## Text\n- **Minimum on-screen sizes, after camera zoom:**\n  - headings 34 px;\n  - key information 22 px;\n  - labels 18 px;\n  - tiny decorative text only when it doesn't need reading.\n  - World size \u00d7 zoom = screen size. A 20 px label at zoom 0.75 shows at 15 px, which is too small.\n- **Fit:** text never overflows its box. Measure with `gg.measureText` and widen the box, wrap, or shrink the font.\n- **Overlap:**\n  - text never overlaps other text or Nexi;\n  - a front-layer prop with text must not sit on Nexi's spot;\n  - Nexi must not stand in front of text the viewer is reading.\n- **Fonts:** Plus Jakarta Sans 800 for display, Inter 600\u2013700 for body, Caveat only for the intro subtitle and deliberately messy samples. Ink is `#1F1F3D`, body text `#4C4C63`, TechNext blue `#3167CA`.\n\n## Cards and props\n- **Card style:** white cards with a soft blue shadow and a 3\u20134 px `rgba(111,160,245,.45)` edge, radius 18\u201334.\n- **Size:** content in a card is generous. If a card has more than five lines of text, split it or zoom in.\n- **Samples:** sample formats (business cards, slides, phones, posts) must look like real, attractive designs at a readable size. A phone shows full proportions (about 1:2) and fits entirely in the frame. Give it its own phone-scale layout (type 18\u201327 px on screen), not a squeezed desktop page.\n- **Sample-format sizes that worked (Brand round 2):** card 340\u00d7200, slide 420\u00d7236, post 300\u00d7375, roll-up 210\u00d7420, signature 440\u00d7150. Text 20 px or more in world px, viewed at zoom 0.82 or above. Make an off-brand and an on-brand version of each.\n- **Labels:** labels on stacked props (crates, cards) sit inside the prop and are large enough to read.\n- **Lines under labels:** draw every connecting line (cables, pipes, routes) before the labels, so no line crosses a label.\n- **Props in hand:** a held prop starts at Nexi's hand (`pocket()`), never gliding in from the world origin.\n- **Sample data:**\n  - Numbers on screen are sample figures and are labelled \"sample\".\n  - Client names are fictional (Bluebay Trading, Lintel & Co., Manila Trading).\n  - Never present a sample as TechNext's own facts.\n\n## Nexi\n- **Moving:** she roams. Every beat moves her somewhere meaningful (fly, hop, ride, carry), and she faces the viewer when she speaks (the default gaze).\n- **Positions:** her target positions are chosen with props in mind. Check mid-flight frames: a flight path must not cross reading text. Route it above or below.\n- **Changing sides:** to get to the other side of a set full of text, she flies up out of frame and drops back in, or rides something (a rail, a plane, an envelope) that keeps her off the labels. `fly()` cuts corners (spring), so leave 250 px of clearance.\n- **Celebrations near the top of frame:** use a small cheer (`excited` + sparks). `bigjump` lifts her about 330 px and clips the hat.\n- **Costume:** one per video, kept on through the outro.\n- **Reactions:** big. Shock, panic and laugh use speed lines, shake and emotes. Use sparks rather than confetti when cards with text are on screen.\n\n## Camera\n- **Grammar:** establishing wide, push-in on a detail, whip pan to the next station, track a moving subject, crane up for builds, pull back for reveals, a dutch tilt for comedy.\n- **Still for reading:** hold the camera still, or nearly so, while a card is read. Move between beats.\n- **Zoom range:** 0.7\u20131.4 for most shots. Below 0.75 the text gets small, so scale world text up for overview shots.\n\n## Viewer quiz\n- **One per video:** 3 s countdown, then reveal and cheer.\n- **The subject stays visible:** the quiz must not cover what it asks about. Use `layout: 'wide'` (top or bottom band) or put the card on the empty side.\n- **Placement:** Nexi stands beside the quiz, never behind it.\n\n## End card\n- **Logo:** centred; \"with Nexi\" at the right slot; the video's badge at the left slot.\n- **Nexi's spot:** one of `top`, `left`, `right`, `below`, `leftlow` or `rightlow`, and a different one for each video in a batch. On the marketing end card the link row spans x 426\u20131500, so the low spots sit just outside it.\n\n## Audio\n- **Music:** one style per topic. Beds sit 10\u201312 dB under the voice, about 5 dB under it in the gaps, measured with `_buslevel.py`.\n- **Timbre:** bright FM or bell tones flood the 2\u20134 kHz voice band. Low-pass them.\n- **Levels:** loudness about \u221217 LUFS; true peak \u2264 \u22122 dBFS (`render.LIMIT` 0.7); voice ratio \u2265 3.5\u00d7. A true-peak miss is usually one sharp effect (`snip`, `jet` under blips). Find it with `qa/_peaks.py` and lower that effect rather than the limiter.\n\n## Added from batch 3 (2026-10-04)\n- **Hats and plates:** a tall hat is part of Nexi's silhouette. Keep the hat top clear of name plates and signs, including during reactions that lift her.\n- **Crossing a labelled set:** leave over the top of the frame and drop back in. Waypoints alone don't stop the spring from cutting through labels.\n- **Labels on contrast:** text never sits directly on a coloured prop; put it on a chip or a plate.\n- **Screen-layer cards:** a card that must be read while the camera shows a busy world (a log, a counter) goes in the `screen` layer over empty sky.\n- **End-card spot by hat:** `below` only with low hats (bow tie, cap, bellhop); tall hats use `left`, `right`, `leftlow` or `rightlow`.\n"},{"path":"reference/engine.md","kind":"md","text":"# Engine reference (film-script API)\n\nA film file is plain JS running inside one closure with every engine helper in scope. World coordinates are px on a 1920\u00d71080 stage as seen with the camera at home; the world can extend far beyond the frame.\n\n## Timeline\n- `say(text, {hold, delay})` \u2192 line `{t0, end, w(word, nth)}`. Speaks in Nexi's robot voice (42 ms/char, pauses at punctuation) and types the caption. `**bold**` highlights. Advances `cur`.\n- `at(t, fn)` schedules at absolute time; `later(d, fn)` relative to now (inside callbacks); `wait(d)` advances `cur`.\n- `everyFrame(fn)` per-frame mover; return `false` to stop. A mover may start other movers.\n- `hud(title)` (define in the film) updates the top-left chapter pill.\n\n## Intro / outro\n- `introSeq({ sub, hud, pills: [[icon, label]\u2026], nx, ny, extra: fn(gg, t) })`, where pills use Odoo app keys (`crm`, `sale`, `account`\u2026) or `L.ICON` names (`globe`, `megaphone`, `spark`, `layers`, `leaf`, `hanger`, `plane`, `train`, `hardhat`, `magnifier`). Put the video's badge in `extra` with `badge(gg, text, Intro.slotL().x, Intro.slotL().y, { col, icon, rot, k, fs, ring })`.\n- `outroSeq({ cta, sub, nexi, extra })`:\n  - `cta: endCtaMkt` and `sub: 'Websites \u00b7 Social \u00b7 Brand'` are for marketing videos. The default is the Odoo end card.\n  - `nexi` is the end-card spot: `top` (960, 250), `left` (300, 520), `right` (1640, 520), `below` (960, 985, peeking up), `leftlow` (300, 770) or `rightlow` (1640, 770). Use a different one per video. Coordinates live in `OUTP` in `engine_head.js`; test with `films/outrotest.js`.\n- The world is hidden (`WORLDOFF`) while the intro logo or the end card is on screen.\n\n## Camera\n- `CAM.{x, y, zoom, rot}`: the world point at the screen centre, the zoom, and the roll in radians.\n- `cam({x, y, zoom, rot}, dur, ease, delay)`, `camWhip(x, y, dur, {zoom, rot})` (fast pan with motion blur and a whoosh), `camFollow(fn, k)` (track `fn()`), `camHome(dur)`.\n- `toScreen(p)` / `toWorld(p)` convert between world and screen.\n- Nexi and the 3D set mirror pan and zoom in 3D, so they render sharp. Keep the zoom between 0.6 and 1.6.\n- Screen-fixed things (cards that must not move with the camera) go in a prop's `screen` layer.\n\n## Nexi\n- `N.place(x, y, z)` teleports her (use it off-screen, then `fly` in on the next frame with `later(0.05, \u2026)`).\n- `fly()` starts from `N.botPx()` and moves her through `N.go`, a spring, so she lags and cuts corners on fast curved paths. Keep 250 px of clearance and about 1300 px/s or slower.\n- `goTo(x, y, z)` moves her on a spring. `fly(points, dur, {e, pose, then})` follows a Catmull-Rom path, `hop(x, y, z, dur, h)` makes an arc, and `orbit(cx, cy, r, turns, dur)` circles a point. z > 0 is closer and bigger; keep z between \u22124 and 4.\n- `N.headPx()`, `N.handPx('L'|'R')` and `N.botPx()` return world px. `N.scale` is the depth scale.\n- Acting:\n  - `E.joy/star/shock/wink/focus/cross/think/love/dizzy(d)` set expressions.\n  - `RX.shock/panic/nope/joy/laugh/faint/cry/huff/love/hmm/dance()` are full reactions.\n  - `N.play(clip)` plays a clip: `bigjump`, `flip`, `wave`, `cute`, `kiss`, `presentBig`, `pointR2`, `pointL2`, `pointUp`, `tapBig`, `tapBigL`, `shockJump`, `excited`, `shrug`, `nope`, `laugh`, `facepalm`, `tantrum`, `panic`, `groove`, `think`, `slam`, `toss`, `tossL`, `hammer`, `salute`, `peer`, `superman`, `water`, `paint`.\n- Costumes: `wear('hardhat'|'conductor'|'pilot'|'glasses'|'strawhat'|'beret'|null)`. New costumes go in `build_core.py` `buildWear()` and are built up front.\n- `look(x, y, d)` makes her look at a point, then back at the viewer.\n\n## Film-local helpers worth copying (they live in the films, not the engine)\n- `pocket(o, hand, dx, dy)` puts a `thing` at her hand, then `grab`s it, so it never glides in from (0,0).\n- `smallCheer()` celebrates without the 330 px `bigjump` (`excited` + star eyes + sparks + `tada`).\n- `one(i, v)` makes a `{i: v}` object for per-index tweens (never tween a whole array).\n- `fitText(gg, s, x, y, fs, col, maxw)` shrinks text to fit a box.\n- `rideRail()` (brand) and the envelope or plane movers (web, enterprise) let her ride something instead of crossing text.\n\n## Names a film must not use (engine globals)\n`CAM CAP CAPHIDE_O CAPX CH CHAPS CHLOCK DESAT E EMO END0 ENV EV EVS FLY FONTS FX GAZE HUD KIT LASTGO LENS N NX PROPS QZ QZP RX SET SHEET SPL SPOT TETH THINGS TICKS TRUCK W WITH ZAPS`, plus `g`, `H`, `U`, `C`, `L`, `UI`, `P`. Reusing one (for example `var CH`) breaks the engine in odd places.\n\n## Props and things\n- `prop({ on: true, back(gg), front(gg), screen(gg) })` draws a layer:\n  - `back` is drawn behind Nexi, `front` in front of her (text there can cover her);\n  - `screen` is fixed to the screen, unaffected by the camera.\n- `thing({ draw(gg, o), layer, x, y, s, rot, a })` makes a world object Nexi can handle:\n  - `grab(o, hand, dx, dy)` glides it into her hand;\n  - `toss(o, x, y, dur, { h, spin, land, then })` throws it;\n  - `holdPose(on)` sets a carrying pose.\n  - Stacks the viewer reads should sit in the `back` layer; switch to `front` when grabbed.\n- `KIT` drawing kit:\n  - `KIT.card(gg, x, y, w, h, {r, sh, edge, fill})` and `KIT.chip(gg, text, x, y, col, bg, fs, {icon, edge})`, which returns the width;\n  - `KIT.person`, `KIT.arrow`, `KIT.ground` (a soft floor shadow), `KIT.stamp(gg, text, x, y, col, k, rot, fs)`;\n  - `KIT.paper(kind)` draws a spreadsheet, slip, invoice, note or mail.\n- Particles and pops:\n  - `L.sparks`, `L.confetti`, `L.hearts`, `L.ring` and `L.emit` for particles.\n  - `L.pop(text, x, y, {follow})` is mapped to the screen and clamped. Never let a pop drift into the HUD.\n- Odoo tiles: `L.drawTile(gg, key, x, y, size, alpha, rot)`. Add new keys to `L.ODOO` in `lib.js`.\n\n## Viewer quiz\n- `quiz({ q, opts, ans, t, side: 'left'|'right'|'auto', y, layout: 'card'|'wide' })`, then `quizReveal()` and `quizOff()`.\n  - The `card` layout is 760 px wide beside Nexi.\n  - The `wide` layout is a 1180 px band. The default y is 700, just above the caption; pass `y: 40` for a top band. Use it when the subject must stay visible.\n  - When the answers don't fit beside the question, the wide band switches to two rows by itself (p25): question on top, answer pills centred below, 216 px tall.\n\n## Effects\n- `shake(k)`, `speedLines()`, `FX.flash = T` (white flash), `FX.mb = T` (motion blur), `FX.desat` + `FX.wipe` (grey-to-colour wipe), `bang(text, col)`, `emote(kind, d)`.\n\n## Odoo screens (Odoo-app films)\n- `P` is the window pose (`SET.PBASE` safe area) and `UI.go(screen)` switches screens.\n- `tap()` becomes `zap()` (a beam plus an outline). Use `spotlight(key|rect)` / `spotOff()` and `lens(key, {k, pos})` / `lensOff()`.\n- `tether(fn|key)` with `TETH.sag` makes the beam run under cards.\n\n## Audio\n- `sfx(name, vol)` plays an effect: whoosh, swoosh, swish, pop, tap, tick, click, type, ding, chime, sparkle, stamp, drop, pick, cash, truck, heart, gasp, boing, tada, magic, holo, rise, buzz, giggle, wah, steam, hmm, dizzy, alarm, ring, clock, whip, slide, chirp, hammer, saw, crane, whistle, chug, bell2, jet, shutter, pour, grow, bee, snip, spray, jingle, plug, flap, splash.\n- `music(cue)` takes `intro`, `main`, `tense`, `after`, `outro` or `end`. The film's preset comes from `audio_t.MAIN` (film \u2192 style). Styles: walk, myth, tip, reorder, tense, before, after, build, rail, jet, web, garden, studio.\n- `MUSIC_GAIN` is set per film. Measure with `_buslevel.py`.\n\n## Added in batch 3 (2026-10-04)\n- **Costumes:** `wear('chef'|'cap'|'knight'|'bowtie'|'goggles'|'bellhop')` (in `build_core.py`, built up front with the others). Heights above the head: chef and knight about +0.6 head units (tall), cap and goggles low, bow tie at the neck, bellhop small and tilted.\n- **End cards:** `endCtaTech` (IoT \u00b7 Apps \u00b7 Networks) and `endCtaAI` (Odoo + AI \u00b7 Automation \u00b7 Chatbots), made by `endCtaRow([[icon, label], ...])`; use with `sub: 'IoT \u00b7 Apps \u00b7 Networks'` or `sub: 'Odoo + AI \u00b7 Automation \u00b7 Chatbots'`. The AI row is wider (about x 353-1567), so low end-card spots collide with it.\n- **Icons (`L.ICON`):** chip, phone, wifi, shield, bot, bolt, bubble, bell, thermo, gear, chefhat, castle, cloche.\n- **Odoo app tiles (`L.ODOO`):** iot, quality_control, maintenance, industry_fsm added.\n- **Music styles:** kitchen (steel pan + swing), field (clav + funk), castle (lute + toms), butler (harpsichord + soft), machine (music box + ticking), lobby (e-piano + lo-fi). Every new tone is low-passed at 2.3-2.8 kHz to stay out of the voice band.\n- **Build guard:** `build_film.py` stops on any film-level `var`/`function` name that the engine also declares (TOTAL is allowed).\n- **Other tools in the renderer:** `thumbs.py` (YouTube thumbnails and 9:16 covers from `films/thumbs.js`), `vertical.py` (9:16 versions: the 16:9 episode inside a branded frame), `deliver_series.py`.\n"},{"path":"reference/qa-checklist.md","kind":"md","text":"# QA checklist\n\nRun this on every contact sheet, before the final render and again on `deliver.py sheet`. Include transition frames (mid-flight, mid-whip, card popping in), not only rest frames.\n\n## Frame audit (each frame)\n- [ ] **Readable text.** Every word I'd expect a viewer to read is at least 18 px on screen and inside its box.\n- [ ] **No overlaps.** No text overlaps other text, Nexi, a quiz card, the caption or the HUD.\n- [ ] **Nexi visible.** She is not hidden behind a front-layer prop, not cut by the frame (unless rising in), and not in a corner.\n- [ ] **Balanced composition.** Nothing parked in a corner, and the frame is not lopsided-empty.\n- [ ] **Quiz.** The subject of the question stays visible, and Nexi stands beside the card.\n- [ ] **Clean intro and end card.** No world props on the logo, the logo is centred, and Nexi is at this video's spot.\n- [ ] **Clean edges.** No leftovers from a previous scene (blueprint slivers, old cards, faded maps) peeking in at the edges.\n- [ ] **Truthful content.** Real TechNext facts are correct; samples are labelled; no invented claims.\n- [ ] **Caption sync.** The caption matches the beat on screen. The first word isn't \"...\", and no line sits blank for over 0.5 s.\n\n## Motion audit (scrub the sheet in order)\n- [ ] **Clean flight paths.** They don't cross the text being read. Route over or under.\n- [ ] **Camera.** Moves happen between beats, it holds while text is read, and there are no jumps.\n- [ ] **Props in hand.** Held props glide into the hand (`grab`); nothing teleports.\n- [ ] **Movers.** Movers launched inside movers run. If Nexi \"freezes\", check that `fly()` was actually called.\n- [ ] **Corner cutting.** Look at frames in the middle of every long flight. The spring cuts corners, so check she clears labels, not just the waypoints.\n- [ ] **First frames of held props.** No prop glides in from the top-left (world 0,0); use `pocket()`.\n- [ ] **Caption line count.** Where content sits low, check the frame during the longest caption (three lines reach y 720).\n\n## Audio\n- [ ] Loudness is \u221216.8 to \u221217.6 LUFS.\n- [ ] True peak is \u22122 dBFS or lower.\n- [ ] Voice ratio is at least 3.5\u00d7 (`qa_av.py`).\n- [ ] Music sits 10\u201312 dB under the voice and 4.5\u20136 dB under it in the gaps (`_buslevel.py`).\n- [ ] If the true peak or voice ratio misses, run `qa/_peaks.py <name>` to see which effect causes it before touching the limiter.\n- [ ] No sound effect drowns a word. Long noise effects (jet, whoosh, crane) go between lines.\n\n## Delivery\n- [ ] `deliver.py copy` reports \"drive OK\" (md5 matched).\n- [ ] The file was sent to the user right away with a short caption.\n- [ ] `sync_source.py` was run after the batch.\n- [ ] The skill was updated: `lessons.md`, rules and the engine notes. The hub skills page was rebuilt and pushed.\n"},{"path":"reference/lessons.md","kind":"md","text":"# Lessons log\n\nAppend a dated entry after every video job: newest at the bottom, one bullet per lesson. When a lesson becomes a rule, also copy it into `design-rules.md`.\n\n## 2026-10-03 \u00b7 Odoo walkthrough + topic series (walk, myth, tip, reorder, beforeafter)\n- **User asks:** cinematic, intro like the website logo animation, a floating \"with Nexi\" tag, robot voice, exaggerated acting, floating Odoo icons.\n- **Fixes to the look:**\n  - Lowered the floor-grid and flare opacity.\n  - Use the plain Odoo logo, never \"Odoo Ready Partner\".\n  - Nexi faces the viewer.\n  - Music upbeat per topic, never loud.\n  - Same intro and outro with a new touch per video.\n- **Audit 1** (the user said \"a lot of design lapses\"):\n  - Badge slots sat above the wordmark.\n  - The HUD plate had to be opaque.\n  - The caption card needed a fixed width.\n  - The Odoo window had to sit in a safe-area pose.\n  - Nexi needed her own lane beside the window: clicks become beams (`zap`), spotlights and a lens replace crop-zooms, `tether` handles drags.\n  - Chapter bands were drawn before Nexi.\n  - The intro exit is a crossfade, not an iris.\n  - Shatter effects were reduced to a two-piece tear.\n  - Report data had to be consistent across screens.\n- **Audit 2:**\n  - Myth/fact cards must not sit in a corner (centred pair).\n  - The logo must be centred in intro and outro.\n  - \"with Nexi\" must look as in the walkthrough.\n- **Audio:**\n  - The voice was buried 14 dB under the music. Fixes: ducking 62 %, voice \u00d71.9, blips \u00d74.6.\n  - Loudnorm needs an alimiter. The walkthrough needs limit 0.7.\n  - Measure with `_buslevel.py`.\n- **Engine:**\n  - Glow-pass mesh lists are cached for 240 frames.\n  - Chip pop-ins must use per-item timestamps, not a tween's end value.\n  - Never tween a whole array.\n  - Avoid leading \"...\" in a caption line: the voice pauses on dots.\n\n## 2026-10-03 \u00b7 Solution videos round 1 (erp, crm, enterprise, web, social, brand)\n- **User asks:**\n  - Nexi roams the whole area.\n  - More dynamic reactions.\n  - Not cornered.\n  - More camera movement and angles.\n  - More interactive.\n  - Analogies, role-play, new and random elements.\n  - Every video unique.\n- **Built engine v2:**\n  - A camera mirrored into 3D, so Nexi stays sharp when zoomed.\n  - `fly`/`hop`/`orbit`, `thing` + `grab` + `toss`, 3D costumes, `quiz`.\n  - A marketing end card.\n- **Bugs fixed:**\n  - Depth must pivot around the camera centre, not the frame centre. Without it, Nexi at z \u2260 0 drifted up to 400 px on long sets.\n  - Costumes must be built before the first frame, or the glow pass blooms them white.\n  - Costume colours need `convertSRGBToLinear`.\n  - World props drew over the intro/end-card logo. Added `WORLDOFF`.\n  - Filtering the mover list while pushing to it dropped `fly()` calls made inside movers.\n- **Staging lessons:**\n  - The quiz card covered the plane: hold the plane mid-air during the quiz and land it on the reveal.\n  - The quiz card covered the logos being counted: added the wide layout.\n  - Nexi stood behind the CTA card: put the CTA beside her, or pop it after she lands.\n  - The stale-lead train was off-camera during its line: place story props where the camera will be when the line plays.\n  - `bellkey` FM music flooded the voice band (voice ratio 2.6\u00d7). Low-passing it gave 4.8\u00d7.\n  - A render died with \"F is not defined\" because an outside process killed Chrome. Rerun.\n\n## 2026-10-03 \u00b7 Solution videos round 2 (user review)\n- **User feedback (verbatim):**\n  - \"some text are overlapping, small and hard to read. maximize the designing of the element\" (social)\n  - \"improve presentation design and camera works. design should be visually appealing and eye catching, even the sample format that showcased\" (brand)\n  - \"fix the mobile design \u2026 0:45 the design are too low\" (web)\n  - \"we dont have malaysia. revise the explanation and make it truthful \u2026 explain technext and do also sample company\" (enterprise)\n  - \"0:23 there some blocking in middle station\" (crm)\n  - \"text overlapping\" (erp)\n  - \"in all outro, let nexi to much in the corner. make sure to make nexi will change location every video\"\n- **Rules added:**\n  - Minimum on-screen text sizes.\n  - Labels fit their boxes.\n  - Nexi never in front of text being read; flight paths route around text.\n  - Stacks the viewer reads go in the back layer.\n  - Story props never park on signs.\n  - The phone sample is fully in frame at real proportions.\n  - Hand-over scenes stay above the caption band.\n  - Truthful company facts: TechNext is SG HQ plus PH and VN teams, 10+ countries delivered, no Malaysia. Use a labelled sample company for demos.\n  - Outro spot per video.\n- **Outro spots tested:** `top` (above the wordmark), `left`, `right`, `below` (under the CTA row), `leftlow`, `rightlow`, all clear of the logo and CTA rows. The old right spot (1712, 610) read as cornered.\n\n## 2026-10-03 \u00b7 Solution videos round 2 (fixes delivered)\n- **What changed per video:**\n  - **ERP:** crates moved to the back layer, away from Nexi, with bigger labels; the slab was widened so every label fits; the stamp fades when the floors land. Nexi switches sides by flying *up and over* the house (out of frame) instead of across the floor text, and throws the go-live scissors. Cable labels are drawn after all cables. End spot `top`.\n  - **CRM:** the Projects train was parked on the middle branch sign. It now waits between stations. The quiz uses the new two-row wide band at the top so the signs stay visible. Small text was bumped to 17\u201322. End spot `left`.\n  - **Enterprise:** the line \"Our group\u2026 Malaysia\" implied TechNext's own group. The film now opens with a TechNext crew card (facts from the live site: Singapore HQ; Philippines hub in Taguig for Odoo development and consulting; Vietnam hub in Ho Chi Minh City for AI engineering; clients in 10+ countries). The demo then uses a \"Sample group \u00b7 illustrative\" (Singapore holding, Philippines and Vietnam companies) with an on-screen tag. There is no Malaysia anywhere. End spot `below`.\n  - **Web:** the phone was a tall strip. It is now a real phone (300\u00d7616, about 1:2) in frame with phone-scale type; the page scrolls inside it to the form. The hand-over scene was raised to mid-frame and enlarged. End spot `right`.\n  - **Social:** full readability redesign:\n    - planter labels on the bed fronts;\n    - a week \u00d7 channel plan instead of a 31-day grid;\n    - a big sample post with the quiz in the top band;\n    - three big sample tiles, a proper gate, a bigger basket and report.\n    - End spot `leftlow`.\n  - **Brand:** five realistic sample formats (card 340\u00d7200, 16:9 slide, 4:5 post, roll-up, signature) in off-brand and on-brand versions. Nexi rides the clothes rail like a zipline so she never covers an asset. A full-frame style guide shows tokens, then components. End spot `rightlow`.\n- **Engine lessons:**\n  - **Film globals must not reuse engine names.** A crew card constant `CH` overwrote the engine's chapter state and crashed `drawChapter`. Check the list in `engine.md` before naming.\n  - **`fly()` follows a spring (`N.go`), so Nexi cuts corners.** Give waypoints 250 px or more of clearance around text, and keep long paths at about 1300 px/s or slower. Check mid-flight frames.\n  - **A `thing` created at world (0,0) glides across the whole frame when grabbed.** Use `pocket(o, hand)` to place it at her hand first.\n  - **Draw labels after connecting lines** (cables, pipes), or a later line crosses an earlier label.\n  - **Three-line captions lift the caption top to about y 720.** Keep lines at 86 characters or fewer when content sits low. The top is about 880 for one line and 835 for two.\n  - **`bigjump` lifts Nexi about 330 px.** Near the top of the frame, use a small cheer (`excited` + sparks) instead.\n  - **When the camera follows, clamp its y** so screen-fixed bars (step bars) don't land on the subject.\n- **Audio lessons:**\n  - True-peak overshoots came from single sharp effects: a `snip` at 0.9, and a `jet` under voice blips. Find them with `qa/_peaks.py <name>`, lower that effect in `out/events-<name>.json` and in the film, then re-mux. Changing `render.LIMIT` trades loudness for peak.\n  - The `qa_av` voice ratio counts whooshes placed between lines (correct placement). Read the gap list in `_peaks.py` before \"fixing\" a low ratio. Web stayed at 2.1\u00d7 for this reason, with music 10.6 dB under the voice.\n- **End-card spots across the six solution videos:** `top`, `left`, `below`, `right`, `leftlow`, `rightlow`. On the marketing end card the link row spans x 426\u20131500, so low spots must stay outside it (x \u2264 300 or \u2265 1640).\n\n## 2026-10-04 \u00b7 Solution videos batch 3: Technology (iot, appdev, network) + AI (odooai, automation, chatbot)\n- **User ask:** one video per item in the technext.asia Solutions menu: Technology (IoT Solutions, App Development, Networks) and AI (Odoo + AI integrations, Workflow Automation, AI Chatbots). Saved to `22. Nexi Solution Videos` as `Technology 1-3` and `AI 1-3`. RAG Knowledge Assistants (the fourth AI menu item) was not in the screenshot and not made.\n- **Worlds used:** Chef Nexi's cold store (chef toque, penguin), Technician Nexi's field visit (cap, cat on the van), Gatekeeper Nexi's castle (knight helmet, rubber duck), Nexi the butler (bow tie, owl, cloche CTA), Inventor Nexi's marble machine (goggles, hamster wheel, calendar-with-a-bite CTA), Concierge Nexi on the night shift (bellhop hat, sleeping cat).\n- **A film `var` re-assigns the engine's variable.** Films share one closure with the engine, so `var FLY = [...]` in odooai replaced the engine's flight state: Nexi never moved and was missing from every frame. `build_film.py` now refuses films that redeclare engine top-level names. The guard also caught `SPL` in brand (it had replaced the spotlight state), `END0` in walk (made a plain assignment) and `badge`/`bang` in thumbs. All renamed.\n- **`L.tween` from an undefined field gives NaN, and the prop silently never draws.** The \"Timesheet in / Invoice drafted\" chips in appdev never appeared. Initialise every field you will tween.\n- **Never tween a whole array** (again): the chatbot's language bubbles were reset with an array tween. Set the elements directly.\n- **Unbalanced `gg.save()`** in leftover code (a save with no restore) leaks canvas state into every later layer. Delete dead drawing code instead of leaving half of it.\n- **Tall hats cover name plates.** The chef toque and the knight plume reach about 230-300 px above Nexi's centre, and `RX.panic` or `excited` lift her another 70-100 px. Plan her y against the plate row, then check reaction frames.\n- **Spring lag drags Nexi across labels even with high waypoints.** In automation she crossed the Draft plate twice. The reliable route over a set full of labels is out of the top of the frame (y at least 200 above the frame top) and back down.\n- **`zap()` only targets Odoo UI hotspots** (`SET.spotPx(key)`). For a tap on a world prop use a ring helper (`tapAt(x, y)`: a tap clip plus `L.ring` and a tap sound).\n- **Dark text over a dark prop vanishes** (the \"Vendor\" label over the mailbox post). Put labels on chips.\n- **Cards that would clutter the world go in the `screen` layer.** The automation run log had covered the Draft station and was cut by the frame edge; a screen-fixed card over empty sky fixed both.\n- **Quiz over a tall subject:** zoom out until the subject fits between the wide band (bottom at about y 256) and the caption (one line: top about 880). In appdev, zoom 0.82 with camera y 424 fit a 690 px phone and the Odoo card.\n- **Typed lines:** quote-led lines (`\"Where's my order?\" I read it live...`) speak fine; only a leading \"...\" makes the voice pause.\n- **Audio:** stamps at 0.6-0.7 were the true-peak source again (iot). 0.45 is enough. MUSIC_GAIN iot .31, odooai .31; the rest of the batch passed at the default .34.\n"},{"path":"reference/film-template.js","kind":"code","text":"  /* ======================= <SERIES> \u00b7 <TOPIC>: <the analogy in five words> (~60 s) =======================\n     One paragraph: the analogy, the role Nexi plays, the beats, the quiz, the call-to-action object.\n     Composition: say where Nexi and the cards live; nothing in a corner. */\n  var TOTAL = 0;\n  var GREEN = '#21A366', GREEN_L = '#E3F5EC', CORAL = '#E0456B', AMBER = '#F3B700', NAVY = '#1E3A6E';\n  var STATION_A = 1200, STATION_B = 2600, STATION_C = 4000;          /* world x of each set piece (the camera travels) */\n  function hud(t) { HUD.chap = { n: 0, title: t }; HUD.chapT = window.T; }\n  function cheer() { EMO = []; N.play('bigjump'); E.star(1.8); var b = N.botPx(); L.sparks(b.x, b.y - U * 0.5, 14, 700, ['#FFD25E', '#fff', '#8FC0FF']); sfx('tada', 0.6); sfx('boing', 0.5); }\n\n  /* ---------------- set pieces (one prop per piece; text >= 22 px on screen at its camera zoom) ---------------- */\n  var A = { a: 0 };\n  prop({ on: true, back: function (gg) {\n    if (A.a <= 0.01) return; var w = 700, h = 420, x = STATION_A - w / 2, y = 200, e = L.back(clamp(A.a, 0, 1), 1.6);\n    gg.save(); gg.translate(STATION_A, y + h / 2); gg.scale(e, e); gg.translate(-STATION_A, -(y + h / 2));\n    KIT.card(gg, x, y, w, h, { r: 26 }); L.text(gg, 'Card title', x + 36, y + 56, 36, C.ink, 800, 'left');\n    gg.restore();\n  } });\n  /* things Nexi handles */\n  var ITEM = thing({ a: 0, layer: 'back', draw: function (gg) { KIT.chip(gg, 'Item', 0, 0, C.blue, '#fff', 24, { edge: 'rgba(111,160,245,.5)' }); } });\n  /* the call-to-action object (unique per video) */\n  var CTA = { k: 0 };\n  prop({ on: true, front: function (gg) { if (CTA.k <= 0.01) return; /* draw beside Nexi's final spot, never over her */ } });\n\n  introSeq({ sub: '<Subtitle>', hud: '<HUD title>', pills: [['crm', 'CRM'], ['sale', 'Sales'], ['account', 'Invoicing']], nx: 720, ny: 600,\n    extra: function (gg, t) { var q = clamp((t - 2.45) / 0.4, 0, 1); if (q <= 0) return; var sl = Intro.slotL(); badge(gg, '<Role badge>', sl.x, sl.y, { col: C.blue, icon: 'hardhat', rot: 0.05, k: L.back(q, 2.2), fs: 34 }); } });\n\n  /* ======================= SCRIPT ======================= */\n  at(cur - 0.05, function () { cam({ x: STATION_A - 150, y: 500, zoom: 1.05 }, 1.2); fly([[STATION_A - 600, 560, 1]], 1.0); });\n  var l1 = say('Opening line with the **hook**.', { hold: 0.4 });\n  at(l1.t0 + 0.1, function () { wear('hardhat'); N.play('salute'); });\n  at(l1.w('hook'), function () { L.tween(A, { a: 1 }, 0.5); sfx('pop', 0.7); RX.hmm(); });\n  /* \u2026 one beat per line: camera move, Nexi move, prop animation, sound \u2026 */\n  var lq = say('Quick! The **question**?', { hold: 0.1 });\n  at(lq.end - 0.3, function () { quiz({ q: 'The question?', opts: ['A', 'B', 'C'], ans: 1, t: 3, side: 'right' }); N.play('think'); });\n  wait(3.2);\n  var lr = say('**B!** Because\u2026', { hold: 0.4 });\n  at(lr.t0, function () { quizReveal(); cheer(); });\n  at(lr.t0 + 1.1, function () { quizOff(); });\n  var lc = say('Call to action. **TechNext** will help.', { hold: 1.4 });\n  at(lc.t0, function () { hud('Get started'); cam({ x: STATION_C, y: 470, zoom: 1.0 }, 1.0); fly([[STATION_C + 330, 560, 1]], 1.0); later(0.95, function () { L.tween(CTA, { k: 1 }, 0.5); sfx('pop', 0.8); }); });\n  cur += 0.1;\n  outroSeq({ nexi: 'left', extra: function (gg, t) { var q = clamp((t - 2.9) / 0.4, 0, 1); if (q <= 0) return; var sl = Intro.slotL(); badge(gg, '<Outro badge>', sl.x, sl.y, { col: GREEN, icon: 'hardhat', rot: -0.05, k: L.back(q, 2.2), fs: 34, ring: 'rgba(33,163,102,.3)' }); } });\n  at(END0 + 2.75, function () { sfx('pop', 0.5); });\n"}],"summary":"TechNext makes cinematic explainer videos starring Nexi, the 3D robot from technext.asia. Every frame is rendered in headless Chrome by the in-house engine at ~/ClaudeWork/nexi-tutorial. Nothing is hand-animated or screen-recorded. A video is a JavaScript \"film\" script: timed captions in Nexi's robot voice, plus props, camera moves and Nexi's acting.","steps":[{"t":"Concept","d":"One analogy or role-play world per video, unique across the series."},{"t":"Script","d":"Write web/films/<name>.js."},{"t":"Build and probe","d":"python build_film.py <name>, then render.py probe."},{"t":"Contact sheet","d":"Render a sheet at every beat and at every transition (mid-flight, mid-camera-move frames too)."},{"t":"Render","d":"render.py mp4."},{"t":"Audio QA","d":"Run qa_av.py and qa/_buslevel.py."},{"t":"Final frame check","d":"deliver.py sheet <name> pulls 12 frames from the encoded MP4."},{"t":"Deliver","d":"Copy the MP4 to the shared drive (md5-checked) and send it to the user straight away."},{"t":"Report","d":"Give a status table across the batch: video, length, state."},{"t":"Keep this skill alive","d":"Log the lessons, promote new rules and republish the hub skills page (see below)."}],"lessons":5,"zip":"assets/skills/nexi-video.zip"},{"id":"animation-vocabulary","name":"animation-vocabulary","desc":"Reverse-lookup glossary that turns a vague description of a web animation or motion effect into its exact term (\"the bouncy thing when a popover opens\" \u2192 Pop in; \"the iOS rubber-band scroll\" \u2192 Rubber-banding). Use when the user asks \"what's it called when\u2026\", or describes a motion effect without knowing its name and wants the right word to prompt an AI or designer with. For naming an effect, not designing or building one.","group":"motion","own":false,"licence":"","home":"","note":"","version":"","invoke":"auto","nfiles":1,"date":"2026-07-31"},{"id":"apple-design","name":"apple-design","desc":"Apple's approach to interface design and fluid, physical motion, translated for the web. Use when building or reviewing gesture-driven UI, spring animations, drag/swipe/sheet interactions, momentum and interruptible transitions, translucent materials and depth, typography (optical sizing, tracking, leading), reduced-motion, or the design foundations (feedback, spatial consistency, restraint) behind Apple-style interfaces.","group":"design","own":false,"licence":"","home":"","note":"Distilled from Apple's WWDC design talks.","version":"","invoke":"auto","nfiles":1,"date":"2026-07-31"},{"id":"emil-design-eng","name":"emil-design-eng","desc":"This skill encodes Emil Kowalski's philosophy on UI polish, component design, animation decisions, and the invisible details that make software feel great.","group":"design","own":false,"licence":"","home":"","note":"Built on Emil Kowalski's design-engineering philosophy.","version":"","invoke":"auto","nfiles":1,"date":"2026-07-31"},{"id":"find-animation-opportunities","name":"find-animation-opportunities","desc":"Search a codebase or UI for places that don't animate but should, and reject everything that shouldn't. Read-only; it proposes motion with exact values, it does not implement it. Use when the user asks \"what could be animated here?\" or wants to \"make this feel more alive\". For fixing existing animations, use improve-animations or review-animations instead.","group":"motion","own":false,"licence":"","home":"","note":"Built on Emil Kowalski's design-engineering philosophy.","version":"","invoke":"auto","nfiles":1,"date":"2026-07-31"},{"id":"impeccable","name":"impeccable","desc":"Use when the user wants to design, redesign, shape, critique, audit, polish, clarify, distill, harden, optimize, adapt, animate, colorize, extract, or otherwise improve a frontend interface. Covers websites, landing pages, dashboards, product UI, app shells, components, forms, settings, onboarding, and empty states. Handles UX review, visual hierarchy, information architecture, cognitive load, accessibility, performance, responsive behavior, theming, anti-patterns, typography, fonts, spacing, layout, alignment, color, motion, micro-interactions, UX copy, error states, edge cases, i18n, and reusable design systems or tokens. Also use for bland designs that need to become bolder or more delightful, loud designs that should become quieter, live browser iteration on UI elements, or ambitious visual effects that should feel technically extraordinary. Not for backend-only or non-UI tasks.","group":"design","own":false,"licence":"Apache 2.0","home":"","note":"","version":"4.4.0","invoke":"auto","nfiles":55,"date":"2026-09-28"},{"id":"improve-animations","name":"improve-animations","desc":"Survey a codebase's animation and motion code as a senior motion advisor, then produce a prioritized audit and self-contained implementation plans for other agents (or cheaper models) to execute. Read-only on source code \u2014 it plans improvements, it does not apply them. Use when the user asks to \"improve the animations\", \"audit the motion\", \"make this app feel better\", or wants a roadmap of animation fixes rather than a review of a single diff.","group":"motion","own":false,"licence":"","home":"","note":"Built on Emil Kowalski's design-engineering philosophy.","version":"","invoke":"auto","nfiles":3,"date":"2026-07-31"},{"id":"marketing-team","name":"marketing-team","desc":"Run TechNext's AI marketing team - Pixel (designer: 3 drips + a 3:4 social post a day), Razel (lead researcher: 20 qualified PH + SG leads a day to the Leads tab) and AJ (social media planner: 1 post a day for Facebook, Instagram, LinkedIn from approved posts). Works the HQ on this PC (http://localhost:3985, hq.py), keeps the AI Marketing HQ sheet and the Marketing Hub AI team page up to date, and keeps improving the agents' skills. Use when the user says \"run the team\", \"run Pixel/Razel/AJ\", \"/marketing-team\", \"make a drip\", \"find leads\", \"plan a post\", \"check the HQ\", or gives the team a job.","group":"design","own":false,"licence":"","home":"","note":"","version":"","invoke":"auto","nfiles":1,"date":"2026-10-03"},{"id":"pick-ui-library","name":"pick-ui-library","desc":"Pick the right library for a given frontend task from a curated, opinionated list \u2014 numbers, OTP inputs, charts, command menus, virtualization, drag and drop, toasts, state, styling, and more. Only runs when explicitly invoked; it does not trigger on its own.","group":"design","own":false,"licence":"","home":"","note":"A curated, opinionated library list.","version":"","invoke":"manual","nfiles":1,"date":"2026-07-31"},{"id":"prototype","name":"prototype","desc":"Build multiple genuinely different versions of a UI piece you describe, rendered behind a visual picker so you can flip through them live and promote the one that feels right. Only runs when explicitly invoked; it does not trigger on its own.","group":"design","own":false,"licence":"","home":"","note":"Builds UI variants behind a live picker.","version":"","invoke":"manual","nfiles":2,"date":"2026-07-31"},{"id":"review-animations","name":"review-animations","desc":"Reviews animation and motion code against a high craft bar derived from Emil Kowalski's design engineering philosophy. Default to flagging; approval is earned.","group":"motion","own":false,"licence":"","home":"","note":"Built on Emil Kowalski's design-engineering philosophy.","version":"","invoke":"manual","nfiles":2,"date":"2026-07-31"},{"id":"web-design-engineer","name":"web-design-engineer","desc":"Build or redesign polished browser-rendered visual artifacts with HTML/CSS/JavaScript/React: pages, dashboards, prototypes, slide decks, animations, UI mockups, and data visualizations. Use for visual front-end creation, design-system exploration, design critique, or explicit browser acceptance / QA of a web artifact. Not for back-end, CLI, non-visual coding, source-to-longform article conversion, or narration-driven click-through video presentations.","group":"design","own":false,"licence":"MIT","home":"https://github.com/ConardLi/garden-skills/tree/main/skills/web-design-engineer","note":"","version":"","invoke":"auto","nfiles":40,"date":"2026-09-28"}]};
